## 6. What this evidence does NOT prove

1. **The result set is not built from this tree.** The 0-divergence reading uses
   `execution-option-c-chainA-fixed3/out/test-results`, produced from a *different* checkout
   (HEAD `613b6b40…`, absent from this repo) that is only *semantically* equivalent to the current
   tree (per the prior source-diff analysis). The shared tree itself cannot produce a reading —
   its own `out/test-results/` is incomplete (6 targets missing). So "0 divergences" is a reading
   over equivalent inputs, not over this worktree's own build products.
2. **No fresh end-to-end regeneration was performed.** All manager runs compare *existing* jsonl;
   I did not rebuild kotlin-f32/rust-f32/swift-f32/haxe-f32 artifacts (~1600-1700 s toolchain run),
   so I cannot independently confirm the added roots compile and gen cleanly today (the guard and
   prior builds are the only evidence for that).
3. **The guard PASS is exemption-conditional** (3 registry.Main exemptions); it does not prove
   unconditional root-set equality.
4. **The allowlist deployment state is untracked** (`?? ` in git per the prior evidence) — I
   verified the file's content and effect in my scratch, but not that any deployment/CI actually
   ships it; the load-bearing property cuts both ways: without the file in place, the run fails.
5. **Coverage note**: the parallel literal change (`1.0e308` -> `3.0e38`) narrows binary64 coverage
   of the "huge finite double" case; my runs confirm the verdict flip but do not evaluate whether
   that coverage loss is acceptable.
6. `f32-roots-fix/FILES.sha256` was not independently re-hashed against the shared tree
   file-by-file (only the manager.js hash and the hxml numstat were recomputed); item-level hash
   equality is **not verified** here.
